<section class="faq bg-grey faqWrapBox">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="headingWrap flexbox">
            <div class="headingsLeft">
                <h2 class="go-BreatherHeading">Frequently Asked
                    <span class="fr-BreatherHeading "> Questions</span>
                </h2>
            </div>
            <div class="headingsRight hide">
                <a href="https://cdn.mastersunion.org/assets/img/newmu/Segmented_Employment_Report_PGP_Rise.pdf"
                    target="_blank" class="gradientButton whiteHover mob-hide">
                    <button class="button iconButton">
                        Employment Report
                        <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="https://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                            <path
                                d="M16.0208 9.41421L7.41421 18.0208L6 16.6066L14.6066 8H7.02082V6H18.0208V17H16.0208V9.41421Z"
                                fill="white"></path>
                        </svg>
                    </button>
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faqWrapper">
            <div class="content-section width-100">
                <div id="tab1" class="content active">

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 active" data-rel="tab-a-1">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading">Who are the faculty? Why should I trust them?</p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-1">Our faculty are practitioners of AI product leaders, deep tech
                            founders, and enterprise transformation consultants who build and deploy AI systems daily.
                            They're not academics studying AI from the outside. They're people who've shipped AI
                            products at scale, led transformations worth millions of dollars, and built companies in the
                            AI space. They teach what they do, not what they've read about.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-2">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> How is this different from IIM/ISB AI programmes?</p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-2"> IIM and ISB programmes are taught by academics who research
                            AI. Our programme is taught by practitioners who build AI. Their programmes focus on case
                            studies and frameworks. Ours focuses on decision-making frameworks plus guided builds that
                            give you fluency. Their placements come from campus drives. Ours come from faculty referrals
                            and project conversions. If you want to understand AI conceptually, go to an IIM. If you
                            want to lead AI products and get hired to do it, come here.

                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-3">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading">What's the community like?</p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-3">You're joining a global network of 76,000+ professionals
                            trained by our faculty across 17+ countries. This includes AI practitioners at Fortune 500
                            companies, startup founders, and transformation leaders. The community continues after the
                            programme — annual refresh sprints, peer learning, job referrals, and ongoing collaboration.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-4">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> Why should I pay $12-18K when I can learn on Coursera for $50/month?

                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-4">You can learn concepts for $50. You cannot: gain
                            decision-making frameworks, work on live projects, get direct access to practitioners who
                            hire, or join a curated community of experienced professionals. Coursera teaches you what AI
                            Product Management is. We give you the frameworks and fluency to actually lead AI products —
                            and the network to get hired.

                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-5">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> Will the curriculum stay relevant? AI moves fast.

                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-5">
                            Yes. All alumni get access to annual refresh sprints covering new tools, frameworks, and
                            industry patterns. This isn't a one-time programme — you stay connected to the cutting edge.
                            We update curriculum continuously based on what our practitioner faculty are seeing in the
                            field.

                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-6">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> Is this a coding bootcamp?
                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-6"> No. This is a leadership programme with enough technical
                            fluency to be credible. You won't become an ML engineer. You'll learn to lead engineers,
                            evaluate architectures, and make decisions. Our promise: you'll know what to ask your team,
                            not how to debug their code.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-7">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading">What's the learning format?</p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>

                        <div class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-7">Each module follows: Framework
                            introduction → Guided build (faculty-led) → Decision lab (you choose between options) → Case
                            review (critique real systems). You understand, apply, decide, and critique. This is how
                            senior professionals learn — not through grinding labs.

                        </div>


                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-8">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> I'm not very technical. Can I handle this?
                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-8"> Yes. We use no-code and low-code tools where appropriate.
                            You'll write some Python, but you won't become an ML engineer. The goal is fluency — you
                            need to understand AI systems well enough to lead teams, evaluate outputs, and make
                            architecture decisions. Not to write production ML code yourself.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-9">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading">What's the real time commitment?
                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-9"> 6 hours of live classes per week (Saturday and Sunday, 3
                            hours each). Plus 4-6 hours of framework study, guided builds, and decision labs. Total:
                            10-12 hours/week. This is intensive but designed for working professionals. If you want
                            something lighter, this isn't for you.

                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-10">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading">What do you mean by 'frameworks, not tools'?

                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-10">Tools change every 6 months. Frameworks compound over years.
                            We teach you decision-making frameworks like Model Portfolio Strategy, Agent Failure
                            Engineering, and AI Unit Economics Canvas — mental models you'll use for the rest of your
                            career. Tools are inputs to these frameworks, not the goal themselves.



                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-11">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading">What's MCP and why should I care?
                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-11">Model Context Protocol is the emerging standard for
                            connecting AI models to external tools and data. It's now under Linux Foundation governance.
                            By 2027, this will be how enterprise AI integrates with existing systems. We're teaching it
                            now because early fluency is a career advantage



                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-12">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading">Why is Unit Economics taught early (Module 4)?
                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-12">Most programmes teach AI economics as an afterthought —
                            'figure out the architecture first, then calculate costs.' That's backwards. In reality,
                            cost constraints should inform architecture decisions from day one. We teach economics early
                            so every subsequent module incorporates cost-aware thinking. This is how senior AI PMs
                            actually work.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-13">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> What's Agent Failure Engineering?

                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-13">Agents fail silently. They don't throw errors, they just
                            give wrong answers confidently. Agent Failure Engineering teaches you to design
                            kill-switches, rollback mechanisms, and partial-success contracts. By 2027, this will be a
                            required competency for anyone deploying AI agents.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-14">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> How do placements actually work?

                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-14">Not through job boards or campus drives. Our faculty hires
                            from their own programmes. When you impress them with your work, you get referred to their
                            networks. Live projects either at your workplace or provided by us - become proof of work
                            that leads to offers. Many participants also transform their current role, taking on AI
                            responsibilities and getting promoted.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-15">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> What if I'm not looking to change jobs?
                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-15">That's common. Many participants want to bring AI
                            capabilities to their current company. The programme gives you everything you need:
                            decision-making frameworks, technical fluency, and production experience. Internal
                            transformation is a legitimate and common outcome.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faqquestionWrap mb30 " data-rel="tab-a-16">
                        <div class="left">
                            <p class="faqHeading"> What's the capstone like?
                            </p>

                            <img src="https://images.mastersunion.link/uploads/23052025/v1/Group35539.svg"
                                class="toggler" alt="">
                        </div>
                        <p class="faqanswer" id="tab-a-16">Real work, not academic exercise. You deliver three things:
                            (1) A production AI system for a real business problem, (2) A 2-page executive case memo
                            that CXOs can actually read, (3) A failure post-mortem documenting what went wrong and what
                            you learned. This proves mature judgment, not just technical skill.

                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<script>
    $(document).ready(function () {
        $('.faqquestionWrap .faqanswer').not(':first').slideUp();
        $('.faqquestionWrap').off('click').on('click', function (event) {
            event.stopPropagation(); // Prevent event from propagating up the DOM tree
            let id = $(this).attr('data-rel');
            $(this).toggleClass('active').siblings().removeClass('active');
            $('#' + id).slideToggle().parent().siblings().find('.faqanswer').slideUp();
        });
    });
    function showApplyNowPopUp() {
        let applyNowPopUp = document.getElementById("ApplyNowPopUpNEW");
        if (applyNowPopUp) {
            applyNowPopUp.classList.add("active")
        }
    }

    

</script>